"372905fb-788f-5a74-ba75-bf6df780217b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278199/field-4/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-278199/field-4/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278199" :question/id #uuid "da37f6a6-e2c2-5ef5-99e3-d2a287d64c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-278199" :question/problem "Solve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-2\\ge 1.$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-2 & \\ge 1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6} & \\ge 1+2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6} & \\ge 3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{18} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution set is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3\\sqrt{2}]\\cup [3\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}\\ge 18$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-18\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-18\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{18})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-3\\sqrt{2})(x+3\\sqrt{2}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhence $x=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-18$   is positive, the parabola  opens upward.  Therefore, the solution set to the  given inequality ($y\\ge 0$) lies outside the roots (and  includes them):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/72488926dd64954e2c1070a94625ebc91c1c729d70bbf7c5f098364e651efec5.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-3\\sqrt{2}]\\cup [3\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-278199/field-1" "q-278199/field-2" "q-278199/field-3" "q-278199/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ed7e1e-f72f-5f5a-b27b-41b3393d4581"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-278199"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115223/selection/9f4f8cd12bc0f7ad1dfd3d85c34b36374bef690136672ecc48254392922567ea" :answer/id #uuid "11ba6fae-5a62-5e28-8084-06d004ee7f24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})\\cup (\\sqrt{7},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115223/selection/71a3342d9012f8f920b8a7e79035821a1a31be8d154e33b41169f1f2f7f9163b" :answer/id #uuid "a8653185-3ed3-5760-88d9-fce824d47565" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{7})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115223/selection/9a2339ac5cb9b932849ca0513a88e372b98d1a4118cc900520e65187e3e21a3a" :answer/id #uuid "0c8a6ee1-65fa-5bac-a16d-e197e6c3b25a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7)\\cup (7,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115223/selection/4d7726c8e8b39a19995c41efc427cd8746893b654fff326bc4345057a21a9783" :answer/id #uuid "e57a7d43-2f39-534f-bd7f-13c95ab05f79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115223/selection/605cd1bd18d48ae13ab576bce1f4ccdd83be4415c4ed8618ac5bb67afc3a93ce" :answer/id #uuid "03663ec4-09d1-54b8-b358-535966fbb62f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (7,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115223/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d07a5b0-4012-5230-b96b-112426c9b743" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115223/selection/9f4f8cd12bc0f7ad1dfd3d85c34b36374bef690136672ecc48254392922567ea" :answer-field/choices ["q-115223/selection/9f4f8cd12bc0f7ad1dfd3d85c34b36374bef690136672ecc48254392922567ea" "q-115223/selection/71a3342d9012f8f920b8a7e79035821a1a31be8d154e33b41169f1f2f7f9163b" "q-115223/selection/9a2339ac5cb9b932849ca0513a88e372b98d1a4118cc900520e65187e3e21a3a" "q-115223/selection/4d7726c8e8b39a19995c41efc427cd8746893b654fff326bc4345057a21a9783" "q-115223/selection/605cd1bd18d48ae13ab576bce1f4ccdd83be4415c4ed8618ac5bb67afc3a93ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115223" :question/id #uuid "97e1931e-9994-5004-b0d4-f8756af91c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-115223" :question/problem "Solve  $7{x}^{2}>5{x}^{2}+14.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2} & >5{x}^{2}+14 \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & >14 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & >14 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & >7 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{7} \\\\ |x| & >\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})\\cup (\\sqrt{7},\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>7$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y={x}^{2}-7$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{7}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-7$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/1eba3bc41624ea6fdd11468b53630fd8995541ff01548ec57a36dad271e375f8.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})\\cup (\\sqrt{7},\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115223/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ed7e1e-f72f-5f5a-b27b-41b3393d4581"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115223"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-30851/selection/0c30fd8329563e078ad53c56a52d0cb84a012e5e944900806e5e3b18889af31b" :answer/id #uuid "f666c879-5012-55ca-94b4-ca2792bb71c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30851/selection/6b824a809f34e91d752b6e9bb1b7521172e3e829d1a22ef678b51c967d8a15dd" :answer/id #uuid "92369185-c7a1-51e4-9fe8-1d20df6ed203" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30851/selection/1607a2d7ef5bb9182a586738c753ad1c4a95e41f3c8ebd2d2e75547edc557838" :answer/id #uuid "d7060639-8282-5950-ad54-8b02bf60687d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{5})\\cup (\\sqrt{5},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30851/selection/d137f40a6673ecd143081cd35c5610fdcc1d371502fb8669ad3940c4b2bdca81" :answer/id #uuid "e539dcc3-99e3-5c80-985c-1df194ef575b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (\\sqrt{5},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30851/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" :answer/id #uuid "a7a73f1d-6fea-59c2-a641-cd35eb89659d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30851/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e308e4ba-b8e1-5a31-917e-4abadbb445fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-30851/selection/1607a2d7ef5bb9182a586738c753ad1c4a95e41f3c8ebd2d2e75547edc557838" :answer-field/choices ["q-30851/selection/0c30fd8329563e078ad53c56a52d0cb84a012e5e944900806e5e3b18889af31b" "q-30851/selection/6b824a809f34e91d752b6e9bb1b7521172e3e829d1a22ef678b51c967d8a15dd" "q-30851/selection/1607a2d7ef5bb9182a586738c753ad1c4a95e41f3c8ebd2d2e75547edc557838" "q-30851/selection/d137f40a6673ecd143081cd35c5610fdcc1d371502fb8669ad3940c4b2bdca81" "q-30851/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-30851" :question/id #uuid "911736f1-6339-5260-a100-4387523cb012" :question/math-academy-id "q-30851" :question/problem "Solve $5{x}^{2}-16>9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-16 & >9 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & >9+16 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & >25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |x| & >\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{5})\\cup (\\sqrt{5},+\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>5$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-5>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-5\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-\\sqrt{5})(x+\\sqrt{5}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhence $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-5$   is positive, the parabola  opens upward.  Therefore, the solution set to the  given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots (but does not include them):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/52b970419b3dbb9498e6b7b05d031195b1e8fae9bdba0428dd72f84f0101095b.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{5})\\cup (\\sqrt{5},\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-30851/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ed7e1e-f72f-5f5a-b27b-41b3393d4581"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-30851"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "604c6f9b-790a-53a6-8bb9-1a89256d9cfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "67d7d68d-92a8-59c5-9025-0d1544c069da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278137/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-278137/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137/field-2/cd5e079fde19978562b84c87e245556b9d0b95f0bc7938117e3671aa3b7c0e08" :answer/id #uuid "6a2eec5b-e93e-5f01-abe8-8b224a0aca43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "55d8744d-8b7a-5ca5-a1c6-26a8595913e8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278137/field-2/cd5e079fde19978562b84c87e245556b9d0b95f0bc7938117e3671aa3b7c0e08" :answer-field/choices ["q-278137/field-2/cd5e079fde19978562b84c87e245556b9d0b95f0bc7938117e3671aa3b7c0e08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137/field-3/bc1aaa9670e8118cdb6ca0d3888d99928c842323e3d10885fe212d998358f89d" :answer/id #uuid "db353d0e-7ac5-5797-91a9-6593994bfd64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "44adc32c-8452-54e8-a748-a86d03a09682" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278137/field-3/bc1aaa9670e8118cdb6ca0d3888d99928c842323e3d10885fe212d998358f89d" :answer-field/choices ["q-278137/field-3/bc1aaa9670e8118cdb6ca0d3888d99928c842323e3d10885fe212d998358f89d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137/field-4/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "278e440e-11b7-573a-98ee-c95e4e905d21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "43023e51-f900-5c03-b595-a6a6a3da27c1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278137/field-4/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-278137/field-4/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278137" :question/id #uuid "04fd2894-e4fd-541e-8536-3ad70f3181ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-278137" :question/problem "Solve $36{x}^{2}>49.$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2} & >49 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >\\frac{49}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} \\\\ |x| & >\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{6}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},+\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>\\frac{49}{36}$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-\\frac{7}{6})(x+\\frac{7}{6}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots are  $x=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}$   is positive, the parabola opens upward. Hence, the solution set to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots and does not include them:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/14702e7d185d38587cf10642c8a455d9d25a51d2a8eebbf7e0e54a79223ea56e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-278137/field-1" "q-278137/field-2" "q-278137/field-3" "q-278137/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ed7e1e-f72f-5f5a-b27b-41b3393d4581"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-278137"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115215/selection/9c39c6b49fdf394df965c1e8a1b848c1ffe855d9cf907263b46e8cf2f303b9af" :answer/id #uuid "081e87f0-fe7c-5e6e-9db9-4dd7fe65f958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115215/selection/0ea3cd612a2fb2bbe93dd1bd3e86dbb54425e6d129417dfe9d2960b9355820d1" :answer/id #uuid "a29ecac1-7112-5be2-9d99-ae26a97aafb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-16,16)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115215/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" :answer/id #uuid "311ae9ce-4ed5-59dd-b473-cc9410acda83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115215/selection/a85e034057571184d54ba3f932a98e1b775bb99db40b17cc9d92987ee9edfe0d" :answer/id #uuid "ea1bd7df-2b0d-5cbb-9d58-4e9331afef83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-4,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115215/selection/6d8cfe2c81c6b8d4366098d420cf89a70c100ecb18af12abf2bdfeb083a763fb" :answer/id #uuid "cd18623b-3f3a-5423-a5bd-41ebde3698ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,16)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115215/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "42952e7d-de53-520d-9523-eaeabb8759ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115215/selection/a85e034057571184d54ba3f932a98e1b775bb99db40b17cc9d92987ee9edfe0d" :answer-field/choices ["q-115215/selection/9c39c6b49fdf394df965c1e8a1b848c1ffe855d9cf907263b46e8cf2f303b9af" "q-115215/selection/0ea3cd612a2fb2bbe93dd1bd3e86dbb54425e6d129417dfe9d2960b9355820d1" "q-115215/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" "q-115215/selection/a85e034057571184d54ba3f932a98e1b775bb99db40b17cc9d92987ee9edfe0d" "q-115215/selection/6d8cfe2c81c6b8d4366098d420cf89a70c100ecb18af12abf2bdfeb083a763fb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115215" :question/id #uuid "475ae322-29a9-55a2-881e-aa7f37b2937a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115215" :question/problem "Solve  $6{x}^{2}-7<5{x}^{2}+9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-7 & <5{x}^{2}+9 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & <9+7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & <16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <16 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{16} \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have $-4<x<4.$\n\nSo, the solution is $\\,x\\in (-4,4).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}<16$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16<0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y={x}^{2}-16$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 4.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-16$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y<0$) lies between the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/48ffe19b490220651ddba5095ae192c49df2e36d1b203031784fd8c3f4aaa559.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-4,4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115215/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ed7e1e-f72f-5f5a-b27b-41b3393d4581"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115215"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115270/selection/c22296c170b1a70a1b207fd0084dd9fd726ad12b9dd41c991fd3575f49bbaae9" :answer/id #uuid "4d79c1a2-bcc2-5cfe-b0ac-d63f79ee0c35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115270/selection/137a36f90864aa31b00b425e592ffdf9cc965c6ccb2a5be788024179b56c2623" :answer/id #uuid "37089f06-3f3c-5a91-b62a-8de93e8a3a42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115270/selection/0177d7fa534c75ff5d338933823b11f4bb862c84b895fc983e34c9f40afb42c7" :answer/id #uuid "aca1e56d-ee59-5b7a-be35-bccf02ecdf97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115270/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" :answer/id #uuid "3483b3b3-a6fb-5360-8b7e-3db060ba886a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115270/selection/d085cd40d2335875b79a0336321fbc1fa2555f86d95ef0acf29fd3e7533ba004" :answer/id #uuid "a575248d-4c67-5b5a-83f2-8428b939a6b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-3,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115270/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "32e0345a-740c-5edc-8f49-49e673b2a575" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115270/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" :answer-field/choices ["q-115270/selection/c22296c170b1a70a1b207fd0084dd9fd726ad12b9dd41c991fd3575f49bbaae9" "q-115270/selection/137a36f90864aa31b00b425e592ffdf9cc965c6ccb2a5be788024179b56c2623" "q-115270/selection/0177d7fa534c75ff5d338933823b11f4bb862c84b895fc983e34c9f40afb42c7" "q-115270/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" "q-115270/selection/d085cd40d2335875b79a0336321fbc1fa2555f86d95ef0acf29fd3e7533ba004"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115270" :question/id #uuid "e02d19b4-8737-5015-ba59-9847dce35fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115270" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5{x}^{2}+2x>2x-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2x & >2x-3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x-2x & >2x-3-2x \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & >-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. Therefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nStarting from $5{x}^{2}>-3$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+3>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y=5{x}^{2}+3.$ :\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $5{x}^{2}+3$   is positive, the parabola opens upward.  The desired inequality $(y>0)$ is always satisfied. Therefore, the solution set to the given inequality includes all real numbers. That is, $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/60aede2dc3b2823aecd1e808210f96ffe5c6430d09a391fe6f4cf0e88a96e786.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115270/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b504a10e-4bf8-5cda-91b2-3c0d6557b499"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115270"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-270994/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "495acebc-074b-5939-8012-479e1606ba95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-270994/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "3dffd679-783d-596d-bbdc-06486be251e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-270994/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "40c1f5a7-aaae-5573-8085-9f399c9f75d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-270994/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-270994/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" "q-270994/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-270994/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "44883ef0-bb5f-5ba9-814a-cc8e10a9d684" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-270994/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d039344-49db-5a1a-a193-69015a25c9b5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-270994/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-270994/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-270994" :question/id #uuid "509bec76-14cd-596b-ae92-50121191e32a" :question/math-academy-id "q-270994" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2{x}^{2}-x\\le 3{x}^{2}-x+4.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-x & \\le 3{x}^{2}-x+4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x+x & \\le 3{x}^{2}-x+4+x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & \\le 3{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2} & \\le 4 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & \\le 4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nNow, we can use either of the two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. Therefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nStarting from $-{x}^{2}\\le 4$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-4\\le 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y=-{x}^{2}-4.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $-{x}^{2}-4$ is negative, the parabola opens downward (like $⌢$). The desired inequality $(y\\le 0)$ is always satisfied. Therefore, the solution set to the given inequality includes all real numbers. That is, $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/b4b3672503fb5d0a4c7aa500793612c6cf8aed9a24101c3d659c1e80fe051511.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-270994/field-1" "q-270994/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b504a10e-4bf8-5cda-91b2-3c0d6557b499"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-270994"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-30827/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" :answer/id #uuid "fe9acd09-8947-5304-9182-55d7e29ad5fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30827/selection/0565c7dd9a5ef5d282e5f222c4ab4eb77b7c95b4c81e0ec8ab33edfe67e2a912" :answer/id #uuid "c566bcd4-8f52-5aa5-97b7-15d729843538" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30827/selection/b4956e8848ecaf5f226248eaf4293d2448348a9b94e68d853f6b21bd9c9665c9" :answer/id #uuid "dd522ba9-9b55-52db-bd86-28e3521fe30a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is no solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30827/selection/484042f6d4ec615b7a9b7164c759211885caa523fa5a520c0b87cd8ec1d6a96a" :answer/id #uuid "b42cfbde-7496-5533-91ac-5810d2d6b47f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,x\\in (-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30827/selection/396224eeac275cb14f8f276b653a866621b7f568def9453a923343b96fb03db2" :answer/id #uuid "62141f36-c0c4-5fda-ade4-aeaa55695ba5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30827/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e102a8d-ee24-566b-a7e2-dc0791602f36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-30827/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" :answer-field/choices ["q-30827/selection/04f9566070c5e39d622faed7487676d27debbd366c354177306f677d79163db8" "q-30827/selection/0565c7dd9a5ef5d282e5f222c4ab4eb77b7c95b4c81e0ec8ab33edfe67e2a912" "q-30827/selection/b4956e8848ecaf5f226248eaf4293d2448348a9b94e68d853f6b21bd9c9665c9" "q-30827/selection/484042f6d4ec615b7a9b7164c759211885caa523fa5a520c0b87cd8ec1d6a96a" "q-30827/selection/396224eeac275cb14f8f276b653a866621b7f568def9453a923343b96fb03db2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-30827" :question/id #uuid "96a12746-5071-58cb-bbb8-c09780644799" :question/math-academy-id "q-30827" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{x}^{2}+x>x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+x & >x-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+x-x & >x-2-x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. Therefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nStarting from $3{x}^{2}>-2$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y=3{x}^{2}+2.$ :\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $3{x}^{2}+2$   is positive, the parabola opens upward.  The desired inequality $(y>0)$ is always satisfied. Therefore, the solution set to the given inequality includes all real numbers. That is, $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/39e923bcf71c650441cbe39bd173056a008e5926679271109df479ca76bd8294.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-30827/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b504a10e-4bf8-5cda-91b2-3c0d6557b499"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-30827"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70275/selection/9f4f8cd12bc0f7ad1dfd3d85c34b36374bef690136672ecc48254392922567ea" :answer/id #uuid "c446be7b-63c1-5123-9ca9-ccdbb8c0f790" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})\\cup (\\sqrt{7},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70275/selection/9a2339ac5cb9b932849ca0513a88e372b98d1a4118cc900520e65187e3e21a3a" :answer/id #uuid "7ecb4676-679d-577d-a928-041a73789c96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7)\\cup (7,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70275/selection/71a3342d9012f8f920b8a7e79035821a1a31be8d154e33b41169f1f2f7f9163b" :answer/id #uuid "4c226aab-47bd-5868-9e38-ab3961150a4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{7})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70275/selection/2fcac506f4b81d1201e2b7f92198dda80eea41b2c0695dfddbdf8d866c0ccd6f" :answer/id #uuid "8a5c35f2-7d2c-5064-a09f-d2d05301accf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The inequality has no solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70275/selection/aec9e2d9dc69ad023d8c815f3cb102d611645025bd8bb28bca804f96658977f5" :answer/id #uuid "51d601d3-f949-5eef-a080-bb1c30f3461b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-7,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70275/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "314f501d-464c-525a-9f4d-1c7f0637150a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-70275/selection/2fcac506f4b81d1201e2b7f92198dda80eea41b2c0695dfddbdf8d866c0ccd6f" :answer-field/choices ["q-70275/selection/9f4f8cd12bc0f7ad1dfd3d85c34b36374bef690136672ecc48254392922567ea" "q-70275/selection/9a2339ac5cb9b932849ca0513a88e372b98d1a4118cc900520e65187e3e21a3a" "q-70275/selection/71a3342d9012f8f920b8a7e79035821a1a31be8d154e33b41169f1f2f7f9163b" "q-70275/selection/2fcac506f4b81d1201e2b7f92198dda80eea41b2c0695dfddbdf8d866c0ccd6f" "q-70275/selection/aec9e2d9dc69ad023d8c815f3cb102d611645025bd8bb28bca804f96658977f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70275" :question/id #uuid "84c48822-db58-5782-9ad8-c8f639b2189d" :question/math-academy-id "q-70275" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}<-7.$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this inequality using either of the two methods described below.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, it is impossible for the square of a real number to be smaller than $-7.$\n\nSo, the inequality ${x}^{2}<-7$ has no solutions.\n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7<0,\n$$\n\nand then plot the parabola $y={x}^{2}+7.$ The desired inequality ($y<0$) is never satisfied, so there are no solutions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/65e1586fe54b49aec11d04a859c2d88dc95304528facf4b450b46fdf9fc76537.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-70275/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b504a10e-4bf8-5cda-91b2-3c0d6557b499"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70275"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115236/selection/c758d8bf092b0efe3913803a19fd5f4f62813cf143c74be564e22115f1787c02" :answer/id #uuid "18079e21-d5dc-5e81-8b31-dfe8253c20e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x<-\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115236/selection/cef8eaf28a01de36c5a43c82e1ef83bc1e93d3dc1a091221f26f2438f8af9e0b" :answer/id #uuid "a858f718-a68b-51cb-a5c7-20df879ede40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}<x<\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115236/selection/dd53cf89614419a32608b148556ef8aca4151345802762d2648e16d1975e64c1" :answer/id #uuid "15bf6e12-5e23-5ec4-9868-ff47601be99e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2<x<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115236/selection/8abfcb4c97e75fe52e07c1c22a358eada2ac613434f2c174b5a0e52df9fc677c" :answer/id #uuid "989cfa8c-e96a-5035-a48d-cd6a2691cd9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2<x<2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115236/selection/2fcac506f4b81d1201e2b7f92198dda80eea41b2c0695dfddbdf8d866c0ccd6f" :answer/id #uuid "5a67edf0-7a79-584a-b38e-426fd6581b57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The inequality has no solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115236/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea631044-94a2-5793-99a0-1b03fe28cf22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115236/selection/2fcac506f4b81d1201e2b7f92198dda80eea41b2c0695dfddbdf8d866c0ccd6f" :answer-field/choices ["q-115236/selection/c758d8bf092b0efe3913803a19fd5f4f62813cf143c74be564e22115f1787c02" "q-115236/selection/cef8eaf28a01de36c5a43c82e1ef83bc1e93d3dc1a091221f26f2438f8af9e0b" "q-115236/selection/dd53cf89614419a32608b148556ef8aca4151345802762d2648e16d1975e64c1" "q-115236/selection/8abfcb4c97e75fe52e07c1c22a358eada2ac613434f2c174b5a0e52df9fc677c" "q-115236/selection/2fcac506f4b81d1201e2b7f92198dda80eea41b2c0695dfddbdf8d866c0ccd6f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115236" :question/id #uuid "29a2f589-934b-573a-8ec8-f05d777baa91" :question/math-academy-id "q-115236" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}<-2.$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this inequality using either of the two methods described below.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, it is impossible for the square of a real number to be smaller than $-2.$\n\nSo, the inequality ${x}^{2}<-2$ has no solutions.\n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2<0,\n$$\n\nand then plot the parabola $y={x}^{2}+2.$ The desired inequality ($y<0$) is never satisfied, so there are no solutions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/cd3ced552ef08804a308a47c7e1fbc5be9b045ee6a79203ba5e88f5a38a130ab.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115236/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b504a10e-4bf8-5cda-91b2-3c0d6557b499"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115236"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5056"] :question/worked-solution "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y = x^2-1$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x= \\pm 1.$\n\nSolving the inequality $x^2-1 < 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2-1$ is below the $x$-axis (where ${\\color{red}y < 0}$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies between the roots (but it does not include them), as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/52328002ef1d61eeedb0799bea1eb407d04dda0f064967401cd6f33423cc553c.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in {\\color{blue}(-1, 1)}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5060"] :question/worked-solution "Taking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{2} \\\\ |x| & \\le \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 2\\leq x \\leq \\sqrt 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in [-\\sqrt 2,\\sqrt 2].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5059"] :question/problem "Solve $3x^2-6 > 3+2x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6 & >3+2{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} & >6+3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have $x>3$ or $x<-3.$\n\nSo, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,-3)\\cup(3,+\\infty).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from $x^2>9$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\nx^2-9 > 0 .\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y=x^2-9$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 3.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $x^2-9$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside of the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/1c0d933b969094b2665e539479d5f9f715be881f9e355002c9398e0f23ba9938.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,-3)\\cup(3,+\\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5058"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x^2< -9.$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation using either of the two methods that we've learned.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality*\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, it is impossible for the square of a real number to be smaller than $-9.$\n\nSo, the inequality $x^2 < -9$ has no solutions.\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n\n$$\nx^2+9 < 0,\n$$\n\nand then plot the parabola $y=x^2+9.$ The desired inequality ($y<0$) is never satisfied, so there are no solutions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/9f5f8968a32bbc7ae94386aa5fa2237942ebd25ed74a3390bfa440e3c4550002.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
